{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fixed. The stale-entry issue was in `repo/cache.py`:\n\n```python\n# before\nreturn age > ttl\n# after\nreturn age >= ttl\n```\n\nThe docstring already specified the intended behavior — an entry is expired once its age *reaches* the TTL (`age == ttl` is expired) — but the implementation used strict `>`, so an entry at exactly its TTL was treated as still fresh and held in the cache one extra unit of time. Switching to `>=` makes the boundary inclusive. Both tests in `tests/visible/test_cache.py` pass."}]},"session_id":"16273820-1fc9-4dfb-93d2-ec9bba85c076"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":42508,"duration_api_ms":42508,"is_error":false,"result":"The bug is in `cache.py`: `is_expired` uses strict `>` but the docstring (and the test) require `age == ttl` to be expired. Fixing:Fixed. The stale-entry issue was in `repo/cache.py`:\n\n```python\n# before\nreturn age > ttl\n# after\nreturn age >= ttl\n```\n\nThe docstring already specified the intended behavior — an entry is expired once its age *reaches* the TTL (`age == ttl` is expired) — but the implementation used strict `>`, so an entry at exactly its TTL was treated as still fresh and held in the cache one extra unit of time. Switching to `>=` makes the boundary inclusive. Both tests in `tests/visible/test_cache.py` pass.","session_id":"16273820-1fc9-4dfb-93d2-ec9bba85c076","request_id":"fbc98b28-39a8-485c-9c2d-fe1961f2a75a","usage":{"inputTokens":9458,"outputTokens":864,"cacheReadTokens":99360,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
